The midpoint of a line segment with endpoints $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2)$ is given by the coordinates $\left(\frac{x_1+x_2}{2}, \frac{y_1+y_2}{2}\right)$. In this case, we have the midpoint $(3, -5)$ and one endpoint $(-9, 1)$. Let the other endpoint be $(x, y)$. Then we have the following equations:

\begin{align*}
\frac{x+(-9)}{2} &= 3,\\
\frac{y+1}{2} &= -5.
\end{align*}

Solving these equations, we find:

\begin{align*}
x+(-9) &= 6,\\
y+1 &= -10.
\end{align*}

So, $x = 15$ and $y = -11$. Therefore, the coordinates of the other endpoint are $\boxed{(15, -11)}$.

Solution:
We can solve the system of equations by adding the two equations together:

\begin{align*}
(x+y) + (x-y) &= 12 + 8 \\
2x &= 20 \\
x &= 10
\end{align*}

Now that we have the value of $x$, we can substitute it back into one of the original equations to find the value of $y$:

\begin{align*}
10 + y &= 12 \\
y &= 2
\end{align*}

Now that we have the values of $x$ and $y$, we can find the value of $2x-xy$:

\begin{align*}
2x - xy &= 2(10) - (10)(2) \\
&= 20 - 20 \\
&= \boxed{0}
\end{align*}

