question:
Expand and simplify completely: \begin{align*}
x\left(x(1+x)+2x\right)-3(x^2-x+2)
\end{align*}

cot_solutions:
Solution:
First, we distribute the terms inside the parentheses:

\begin{align*}
x\left(x(1+x)+2x\right)-3(x^2-x+2) &= x(x+x^2+2x)-3(x^2-x+2) \\
&= x^2(x+1)+2x^2-3x^2+3x-6
\end{align*}

Now, we combine like terms:

\begin{align*}
x^2(x+1)+2x^2-3x^2+3x-6 &= x^3+x^2+2x^2-3x^2+3x-6 \\
&= x^3+0x^2+3x-6
\end{align*}

So, the expression simplifies to:

$$\boxed{x^3+3x-6}.$$The answer is: x^3+3x-6

