We can use polynomial long division to divide $6x^3+9x^2-17x+11$ by $2x^2+5x-1$:

\[
\begin{array}{c|cc cc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{3x} & -1 \\
\cline{2-5}
2x^2+5x-1 & 6x^3 & +9x^2 & -17x & +11 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{6x^3} & +15x^2 & -3x \\
\cline{2-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0} & -6x^2 & -14x & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & -6x^2 & -15x & +3 \\
\cline{3-5}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 0 & x & +8 \\
\end{array}
\]

So, the quotient is $3x-1$ and the remainder is $x+8$. The sum of the quotient and remainder is $(3x-1)+(x+8) = \boxed{4x+7}$.

