\begin{align*}
N &\equiv 5 \pmod{7}, \\
N &\equiv 10 \pmod{11}, \\
N &\equiv 10 \pmod{13}.
\end{align*}

First, we find the product of the moduli: $N' = 7 \cdot 11 \cdot 13 = 1001$. Next, we compute the individual terms:

\begin{align*}
N_1 &= N' \cdot \frac{1}{7} \cdot 1 = 143, \\
N_2 &= N' \cdot \frac{1}{11} \cdot 1 = 91, \\
N_3 &= N' \cdot \frac{1}{13} \cdot 1 = 77.
\end{align*}

Now, we can find the solution $N$ by computing the sum of the products of the remainders and the individual terms:

\begin{align*}
N &= 5 \cdot 143 + 10 \cdot 91 + 10 \cdot 77 \\
&= 715 + 910 + 770 \\
&= 2395.
\end{align*}

However, we want the largest integer less than $2010$ that satisfies the given conditions. Since $2395 > 2010$, we can find the largest multiple of $N'$ that is less than $2010$:

\begin{align*}
\left\lfloor \frac{2010}{1001} \right\rfloor &= 2.
\end{align*}

